GraphQL Scanner — detects introspection exposure, flags sensitive-sounding queries/mutations for review
# GraphQL Scanner

Checks a GraphQL endpoint for the most common real-world
misconfiguration: **introspection left enabled in production**. If
introspection succeeds, an attacker gets the entire schema for free —
every type, query, mutation, and field — normally the hardest part of
testing an unfamiliar API. Also flags query/mutation names that sound
sensitive (`delete`, `admin`, `role`, `password`, etc.) for manual
follow-up.

## ⚠️ Authorized use only
Only use against endpoints you own or have explicit written
permission to test.

## Requirements
```
pip install requests
```

## Usage
```bash
# Basic scan
python graphql_scanner.py --url https://target.com/graphql

# With an auth header (some APIs only expose introspection to logged-in users)
python graphql_scanner.py --url https://target.com/graphql --header "Authorization: Bearer xyz"

# List every query/mutation name, not just sensitive-looking ones
python graphql_scanner.py --url https://target.com/graphql --show-all-fields
```

## How it works
1. Sends the standard GraphQL introspection query (`__schema`) as a
   POST request.
2. If the server returns the full schema, introspection is enabled —
   flagged as high severity, since it hands over the API's entire
   attack surface.
3. If the server returns a GraphQL error instead (`"errors"` with no
   `__schema` data), introspection is correctly disabled.
4. Every query/mutation field name is checked against a list of
   sensitive-sounding keywords (`delete`, `admin`, `role`, `password`,
   `token`, `bypass`, `internal`, etc.) — these get surfaced first
   since they're the highest-value targets for follow-up manual
   testing (auth checks, IDOR, mass assignment).

## Notes
- A disabled introspection response isn't a guarantee of security —
  it just means you'll need to build the schema manually (e.g. from
  client-side JS bundles, API docs, or guessing common field names).
- Finding a sensitive-sounding mutation via introspection doesn't
  confirm it's exploitable — it tells you where to focus manual
  authorization testing next (does `deleteUser` check that the caller
  owns the account being deleted?).

## Status
Part of a personal 100-tool security scripting project. Verified
against two mock GraphQL endpoints: one with introspection enabled
(correctly extracted schema, correctly flagged `deleteUser`,
`updateUserRole`, and `adminGetAllUsers` as sensitive) and one with
introspection disabled (correctly reported safe).

